analysis:
The only changed file is scripts/test-darkbloom-route.py, a CI test script that is not shipped in the provider. The PR adds an HOURLY constant (head line 136) for ci-dev-hourly.yml, whitelists it in test_every_workflow_is_classified (lines 709-710), and adds test_hourly_full_run_only_dispatches_ci_dev_on_dev (lines 959-977). The new test asserts that the workflow:
- triggers only on schedule and workflow_dispatch, with cron '7 * * * *';
- sets top-level permissions to {};
- runs one job on ubuntu-24.04 with only actions:write and contents:read;
- contains no uses:, secrets., checkout, id-token, self-hosted or darkbloom- strings;
- has exactly one `gh workflow run` command, targeting ci-dev.yml on dev;
- skips dispatch while an earlier workflow_dispatch run is unfinished.

These assertions tighten the CI guardrails; they do not weaken them. Adding HOURLY to the classified set means the workflow also falls under the existing check at lines 913-918, which forbids Darkbloom self-hosted labels in every workflow except the router and watchdog. The unrouted release and credentialed workflows still keep literal runners.

The new workflow file ci-dev-hourly.yml is not in the supplied evidence, so I could not check that it matches the test's assertions. Nothing here touches the provider runtime, the attestation or App Attest paths (TB-009, TB-011), prefix-cache storage (T-041), keys, or consumer data. I found no regression in the supplied evidence. The remaining question is whether ci-dev-hourly.yml, which the PR presumably adds, is correct. The test is designed to catch drift in that file, and it is outside this batch.
